## Introduction

I have spent much of my life trying to understand what it means to be alive.

I have looked for God in thought and prayer, in love and desire, in beauty, suffering, failure, silence, and in the strange movements of my own mind. I have looked in philosophy and religion, in the lives of others, and in those moments when the ordinary world suddenly seems transparent to something immeasurably greater than itself.

What follows is what I have come to see.

I do not offer it as revelation. I do not claim authority for it, and I do not imagine that I have discovered what wiser people have failed to discover. Much of what is written here has been said before, in other languages, through other traditions, under other names. Where I have found something true, I suspect it is because I have encountered a truth that was already waiting for me.

This book is therefore a confession more than a proof.

It begins not with an invisible object that must be imagined, but with the immediate actuality of Being. Before I can affirm or deny anything, something is. I cannot stand outside that actuality to examine it, for the examination itself occurs within it. I call the inexhaustible Ground of this existence God. What I further confess concerning God’s goodness, consciousness, and relationship to creation must be distinguished from that first recognition.

Its central confession is simple:

There is only God as ground.

Everything else follows from my attempt to understand what that might mean without denying the reality of the world, the dignity of the individual, the freedom of the creature, the reality of suffering, or the irreducible importance of love.

I have tried to resist two temptations.

The first is to make the creature independent of God: a little sovereign standing apart from Being, possessing itself absolutely and owing its existence to no deeper Ground.

The second is to dissolve the creature into God: to make individuality an illusion, suffering finally unreal, love of particular persons a lesser understanding, and the human being something that must eventually disappear into the Absolute.

I cannot accept either.

I believe the creature is real.

I believe you are real.

I believe I am real.

I believe our distinction matters.

And I believe none of us possesses the source of our own being.

This book is my attempt to hold these truths together.

It is also an attempt to understand something more personal and difficult: how a creature can go wrong while remaining good in its being; how we can deceive ourselves without becoming worthless; how responsibility can be real without becoming condemnation; how suffering can remain genuinely terrible without requiring us to condemn life itself; how we can love another deeply without possessing them; and how we might surrender the demand to be our own Ground without surrendering the particular person we were given to be.

I have not arrived at these thoughts without contradiction.

Some of what is written here was learned through joy. Some was learned through suffering. Some emerged from contemplation. Some came through mistakes I would rather not have made. Some appeared first in dreams, images, stories, and symbols long before I could give them philosophical language.

I therefore distrust any philosophy that makes the philosopher look too clean.

If these pages contain truth, that truth does not make their author innocent.

A person may understand pride and remain proud.

A person may write beautifully about love and still fail to love well.

A person may understand attachment and still cling.

A person may speak of truth while hiding from something true.

Knowing the name of the sickness is not the same as being healed.

For that reason, the philosophy offered here must remain vulnerable to experience.

If grief contradicts one of my beautiful conclusions, I must listen to grief.

If love exposes selfishness that my philosophy has rationalized, I must listen to love.

If another person tells me, “That is not how I experienced you,” I cannot dismiss that testimony merely because it does not fit the story I have constructed.

Reality is not obligated to preserve my philosophy.

If I am wrong, I want reality to correct me.

Indeed, if there is only God as Ground, then intellectual humility is not optional. I do not stand outside Being and look down upon it. I look from here—from one finite life, through one history, one body, one mind, one field of experience.

I may know something truly without knowing it completely.

There are places in this book where I believe reason can proceed with confidence. There are others where I can only confess what seems true to me. And there are still others where the only truthful answer I know is:

I do not know.

I have tried to preserve those boundaries.

I have not always succeeded, and the reader should not surrender judgment where I have failed to exercise mine.

Nothing in these pages should be used to deny another person’s suffering, to excuse cruelty, to sanctify domination, or to tell the wounded that their wound is secretly good.

Nothing here should be used to make another human being expendable.

And nothing here should become a reason to stop looking.

A philosophy of One Ground would betray itself if it became another way for the individual mind to declare itself sovereign.

So I offer this work without asking the reader to believe it.

Test it.

Question it.

Bring your experience against it.

Bring your suffering against it.

Bring philosophy, science, religion, psychology, and the testimony of other lives against it.

Most importantly, bring it against life.

Where it fails, let it fail.

Where it causes us to see another person less clearly, let it be corrected.

Where it makes us crueler, more self-important, more contemptuous, more certain of what we cannot know, or less capable of love, something has gone wrong.

But where it helps us become more truthful, more humble, more responsible, more courageous, more capable of receiving ourselves without enthroning ourselves, and more capable of loving another without possessing them, perhaps it has touched something real.

I do not know how much of what follows will survive such testing.

I hope whatever is false will not.

What I want is not the preservation of my philosophy.

I want the truth.

And if the central confession of this book is true, then truth does not finally belong to me.

I belong to it.

