## The Immediate Ground

Before I ask what exists, I must recognize that something is.

Before I can question the world, doubt my senses, conceive of God, or deny God, there is Being.

Even my doubt is.

Even my denial is.

I cannot stand outside Being to examine it, for I must be in order to examine anything at all.

This is not a conclusion reached at the end of an argument. It is the immediate actuality within which every argument begins.

I am.

But I am not the whole of what is.

I did not bring myself into existence, and I do not sustain myself by an independent act of will. I find myself already here, within a reality I neither created nor comprehend in its fullness.

Being is intimate. It is not distant from me, for my own existence participates in it.

Being is immanent. Nothing that exists stands outside the actuality of Being.

Being is transcendent. No particular creature, thought, sensation, or world of experience can exhaust it.

I am in it.

It is in me.

It is beyond me.

This is what I call the Ground.

And this Ground I call God.

I do not thereby claim to have comprehended the nature of God. I have not discovered the hidden constitution of the Absolute, nor do I imagine that naming Being gives me possession of it.

The name is not the thing named.

I begin with what is immediately given, and from there I inquire into what it means to live.

I confess that the Ground is good. I confess that consciousness is fundamental to its nature. I confess that love is the proper fulfillment of our relationship with Being.

These further claims must not be confused with the immediate recognition from which the inquiry begins. They are convictions to be examined, contemplated, challenged, and lived.

I ask the reader neither to accept them blindly nor to reject them merely because they exceed what can be demonstrated with certainty.

Enter into the inquiry.

Look and see.

Does recognizing the dependence of your existence diminish your pride?

Does contemplating the inexhaustibility of Being awaken wonder?

Does recognizing the reality of another creature enlarge your compassion?

Does accepting responsibility without self-condemnation make you more capable of correcting what you have done?

Does the practice of love disclose something that argument alone could not reveal?

Does life become more abundant?

These questions cannot be answered merely by agreeing with the words.

They must be answered in living.

The purpose of this philosophy is not to explain away the mystery of existence, but to enter into a more truthful relationship with it.

To see more clearly.

To love more fully.

To alleviate suffering where we can.

To refrain from causing needless suffering.

To participate consciously and courageously in the flourishing of life.

The Ground is not waiting to be brought into existence by our understanding.

It is already here.

The question is how we shall live within it.

