## Prologue

There is only God as ground.

God is the ground of being, and leans on nothing. Nothing else is a ground.

Creation is real. It is not a dream, and it is not a second God. The world is God’s being given form as real, particular, dependent beings.

There are not two kinds of being, God’s being and another from which the world was fashioned. There is one Being, and many real forms, many relations, many lives, many centers of experience, many centers of agency.

Creation is God given as dependent form.

Creation is not God as Absolute Ground.

Creation is not other than God.

Creation is not God, because no creature, no form, no world, no totality of forms is the Absolute Ground. Creation is not other than God, because there is no second substance from which anything could be made.

Two words must be held apart from the start.

Ground names what God is to the world: that by which anything is at all. The Ground is immutable.

God in relationship is not therefore an additional being standing beside the Ground. The Ground is absolute; the relationships of creatures are real and changing. I leave open the possibility that God may act intentionally within those relationships, including toward the relief of suffering. Such action need not imply that the Ground itself changes. I do not claim to know the inner manner of divine intention, or to identify with certainty which events express it.

Substance, in this book, means only this: there is no second stuff. It does not mean that God is a material that gets reshaped into things, as clay is reshaped by a potter.

Forms change. Relations change. Lives begin and end. The Ground that gives them being does not change by their changing, as light is not altered by what it falls upon.

This is confessed, not proved. Where this book confesses rather than knows, it says so.

A necklace is gold, but its work is to be a necklace. That it is gold does not make the necklace unreal. That the necklace exists does not exhaust what gold is. What a thing is made of, and what a thing is, are different questions.

The analogy limps in one place. Gold is rearranged when a necklace is made. The Ground is not. The image teaches only this: being made of one Being does not make a thing less real.

So with the creature.

The tree is truly a tree. The person is truly a person. The stone is truly a stone. Their reality does not require them to be made from something other than God. Their distinction lies in form, relation, function, agency, dependency, and causal difference.

The creature is God’s being as this particular dependent life.

It did not found itself. It does not sustain itself from its own side. It cannot truthfully say:

I am the Ground.

The difference is dependency.

God is from no other.

The creature is from God.

God depends upon nothing.

The creature depends upon God for everything it is.

Its particularity is real. Its agency is real. Its otherness from other creatures is real.

But particularity is not separation, and agency is not self-origination.

There is only God as ground, and there are many real dependent others.

