## I. Ground and Creation

### Creation

Creation is eternal in its dependence upon God.

This concerns ontological dependence, not a proved absence of a first moment in time. Whether time has a first moment is a different question.

Creation is not first of all an event that happened once, far back. There need not have been a first moment in which God existed without creation and then brought creation into being.

An eternal universe would be no less created than a universe with a beginning. A causal process may run without a first member and still have no ground in itself.

God is not the first link in an infinitely long chain of causes.

God is the Ground by which there is a chain at all.

God is not earlier than creation.

God is deeper than creation.

God is.

Creation becomes.

Being eternally gives itself as form. Forms arise, meet, change, divide, join, perish, and arise again. Bodies flower from the universe as roses flower, in their season, from the living plant.

Creation is not a finished artifact left behind by its maker.

It is the eternal flowering of dependent form.

Novelty belongs to creation.

A child can come into being who did not exist before as this child.

A choice can occur that had not occurred before as this creature’s choice.

A star can ignite.

A body can perish.

A causal path can divide.

These are real changes. They are changes of finite form, relation, and experience. They do not make the Ground more or less what the Ground eternally is.

God does not become more God by creating.

God is not completed by creation.

God is not diminished by refusal.

God is not perfected by communion.

The Ground is immutable.

The dance moves.

### Eternal Knowledge

One premise must be stated rather than smuggled in.

God’s eternity is not a very long time. It is not in time at all. God does not stand at some moment looking forward to what has not yet happened. All that creation becomes is present to God at once.

This is assumed here, not proved. What follows depends on it.

God is eternally knowing.

God does not pass from ignorance into knowledge as creation unfolds.

This knowledge does not answer for the creature.

Take any creature, and call her Violet.

If Violet freely says No, God eternally knows Violet freely saying No.

If Violet freely says Yes, God eternally knows Violet freely saying Yes.

God’s knowledge does not stand before the creature as a prediction that reality is forced to obey. God knows reality as it truly is.

A free act, if free, is eternally known as free.

To know that truth is not to cause it.

God’s eternal knowledge of Violet’s No does not utter the No.

It does not stand behind the act as a prior necessity the act is forced to obey.

The knowledge makes the act no more necessary than the act makes itself.

The creature still answers.

The Ground does not speak the refusal.

Something can therefore be genuinely new in creation without becoming newly known to God.

The dancers truly dance.

God eternally knows the dance.

