## II. The Creature

### Consciousness and Awareness

God is Consciousness: undifferentiated, self-illuminating, without creaturely attributes, and good.

God does not possess consciousness as one attribute among others. God is not dead substance waiting for matter to become conscious.

Nor does undifferentiated Consciousness mean an infinitely enlarged individual mind. At the level of the Ground there is no standpoint, no boundary, no location, no private perspective.

Consciousness is self-illuminating. It does not need a thought in order to be luminous.

There is no necessary I am within Consciousness itself.

The I arises with differentiation.

Two words will be kept apart throughout.

Consciousness, capitalized, names the Ground as luminous. It is not absent from anything that is.

Awareness names creaturely experience: conditioned, arising with this form, belonging to this being. It requires a finite organization capable of experience.

A stone is truly a stone. A tree is truly a tree. A person is truly a person. All arise within Consciousness. All bear the seed, because none stands outside it.

Yet this does not require us to claim that every form has awareness. Whether forms lacking the organization of experiencing beings have some primitive awareness is unknown.

We need not invent an answer.

Organization matters.

A being is a differentiated finite form with the organized potential for awareness. That potential may remain when awareness is absent. In dreamless sleep, under anesthesia, in coma, the being does not cease merely because present experience ceases.

The seed remains.

The seed is participation. It is not a moral status, and it is not already a subject.

Remote causal potential is not the same as organized experiential potential. A rock and an embryo need not be treated as morally identical merely because both are forms of the same Being. An organized developmental path may move, barring catastrophe, toward the capacities of an experiencing being, while a stone stands unimaginably far from such organization.

Where the relevant thresholds lie may be unknown. Culpability follows the truth available to the creature and its genuine capacity to understand it, not whatever belief it prefers to hold.

Within an experiencing being, five dimensions converge:

**Form:** the body and the material world, including the sense organs and the mind as a sense organ.

**Feeling:** the pleasant, unpleasant, or neutral tone of experience.

**Perception:** the recognition, labeling, and interpretation of what is encountered.

**Mental formations:** thoughts, emotions, intentions, beliefs, habits, and patterns of reaction.

**Awareness:** the conditioned knowing by which sensory and mental events are known in this being.

The fifth is not simply another object beside the first four. It is where Consciousness becomes particular.

The mind can think, remember, imagine, judge, fear, desire, intend, and construct an image of itself.

Awareness illuminates all of these.

A false thought and a true thought are both illuminated. A cruel intention and a loving intention are both illuminated.

The light does not become false because it illuminates falsehood. The mind can become false. They are not the same thing.

The sickness belongs to the organization of perception, formation, craving, clinging, intention, and action. Consciousness is not corrupted by what appears within it.

One sun may be reflected in a thousand pools.

The reflection does not produce the sun. Neither is the reflection unreal. Its appearance belongs to this pool: its place, its depth, its movement, its history.

Change the pool and the reflection changes.

So with the creature.

Consciousness is God’s.

Awareness is the creature’s.

God does not become conscious through creatures.

Consciousness becomes particular through creatures.

Embodiment gathers the immense field of creation into a single point of view.

I see from here.

I suffer here.

I encounter you from here.

The window is the creature.

But it is misleading to say God is secretly the creature looking through the window. God is the Ground of the creature’s awareness. The creature is the subject of the experience.

God knows Violet’s experience completely. Violet experiences it.

God grounds Violet’s agency. Violet acts.

Dependency does not erase possession in the creaturely sense. Violet’s experience is genuinely Violet’s, though she does not possess her being as an independent Ground.

There is no hidden sixth substance behind the five that must be called Violet.

Violet is the living differentiated unity in which these occur.

The subject is real.

Subjectivity is a mode of experience available to the subject. Therefore subjectivity can cease without the subject ceasing to be.

### Individuality and Moral Trajectory

Finite individuality arises through causal differentiation.

The creature is not a private piece of Being cut away from God. Nor is individuality produced by inserting an independently existing soul into a body.

The tree is this tree, and the stone is this stone, by the causes that gathered them.

Neither has a moral trajectory.

Personal continuity is a narrower line within that differentiation.

Every creature exists within an immense causal field. Air enters the lungs. Food becomes flesh. Language passes between minds. One life alters thousands of others.

If every causal effect carried personal identity with it, the individual would dissolve into the causal totality of the universe.

Personal continuity follows a narrower line: the moral trajectory.

The moral trajectory is not merely a record of ethical decisions. It is the continuing orientation of an experiencing being toward truth and Good: its ignorance and recognition, attachment and release, intention, refusal, choice, consequence, culpability, grace, and consent.

The creature is not a container holding a self.

It is a living continuity.

The trajectory begins from here: this living being, this field of experience.

It continues by two marks.

**Contiguity:** what follows arises from it without gap.

**Congruence:** what follows takes up its condition as the starting point of its own experience and answer, not as something met from outside a field of experience already underway.

The spilled glass is contiguous but not congruent. It has no field of experience at all.

A stranger may resemble me closely, but is not contiguous.

My child is touched by my condition, but her field of experience arises from causes of its own. My condition reaches her from outside it, as influence.

Causes radiate.

Condition is carried.

The trajectory is the line along which it is carried.

These marks are not fully free of circularity. They describe what continuity looks like from inside rather than deriving it from something more basic. They are offered as a description, not a proof.

An antique chair sent for restoration undergoes innumerable alterations. Wood is removed. Tools wear. The chair itself changes. Yet what returns is the chair transformed, not every effect produced during its restoration.

So with the creature, though a person’s continuity is deeper than an artifact’s.

As with the chair, some cases may have no sharp answer.

Continuity is what matters, not a sharp line.

Matter may change. Memory may change. Body may change.

The moral trajectory continues.

Memory is evidence of continuity. It is not the ground of continuity.

I may truthfully say:

I do not remember doing this.

That does not necessarily entail:

I did not do this.

The past may remain present in disposition, attachment, orientation, vulnerability, and form, without being present as recollection.

This book also confesses something it cannot know: that the trajectory is older than this life.

On that confession, the infant does not become a moral being only when it acquires language. Its ability to understand is new. Its trajectory is not. Birth would then not be absolute beginning, as death would not be absolute ending.

This is a confession, not a finding. It is taken up again in Part VI, with the hazard it carries.

The past explains how the creature arrived here.

The moral demand occurs here.

